{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nLittle Johnny Bubbles enjoys spending hours in front of his computer playing video games. His favorite game is Bubble Strike, fast-paced bubble shooting online game for two players.Each game is set in one of the N maps, each having different terrain configuration. First phase of each game decides on which map the game will be played. The game system randomly selects three maps and shows them to the players. Each player must pick one of those three maps to be discarded. The game system then randomly selects one of the maps that were not picked by any of the players and starts the game.Johnny is deeply enthusiastic about the game and wants to spend some time studying maps, thus increasing chances to win games played on those maps. However, he also needs to do his homework, so he does not have time to study all the maps. That is why he asked himself the following question: \"What is the minimum number of maps I have to study, so that the probability to play one of those maps is at least $$$P$$$\"?Can you help Johnny find the answer for this question? You can assume Johnny's opponents do not know him, and they will randomly pick maps.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class C {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        C solver = new C();\n        solver.process();\n    }\n    \n    public void process() throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader stdin = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String[] firstLine = stdin.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(firstLine[0]);\n        double d = Double.parseDouble(firstLine[1]);\n        \n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            double numerator1 = 1.5 * i * (n - i) * (n - i - 1);\n            double numerator2 = 3.0 * i * (i - 1) * (n - i);\n            double numerator3 = 1.0 * i * (i - 1) * (i - 2);\n            double denominator = n * (n - 1) * (n - 2);\n            \n            double probability = (numerator1 + numerator2 + numerator3) / denominator;\n            \n            if (probability + 1e-8 >= d) {\n                System.out.println(i);\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class C {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        C solver = new C();\n        solver.process();\n    }\n    \n    public void process() throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader stdin = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String[] firstLine = stdin.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(firstLine[0]);\n        double d = Double.parseDouble(firstLine[1]);\n        \n        // Handle edge cases\n        if (d <= 0.0) {\n            System.out.println(0);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        // Try each possible number of maps to study (from 0 to n)\n        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n            double probability = calculateProbability(n, i);\n            \n            if (probability + 1e-8 >= d) {\n                System.out.println(i);\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    private double calculateProbability(int n, int studied) {\n        // Total ways to choose 3 maps from n\n        long totalWays = (long)n * (n-1) * (n-2);\n        \n        // Count favorable cases where at least one studied map is selected\n        // and not discarded by both players\n        \n        // Case 1: Exactly 1 studied map in the 3 selected\n        // - Studied map must be picked by at least one player (not discarded)\n        // - Opponent picks randomly, so we need to compute probability that studied map survives\n        \n        // Case 2: Exactly 2 studied maps in the 3 selected\n        // - At least one studied map must survive\n        \n        // Case 3: Exactly 3 studied maps in the 3 selected\n        // - At least one studied map must survive\n        \n        if (studied == 0) return 0.0;\n        \n        double favorable = 0.0;\n        \n        // Number of ways to choose k studied maps and (3-k) unstudied maps\n        for (int k = 1; k <= Math.min(3, studied); k++) {\n            if (3 - k > n - studied) continue; // Not enough unstudied maps\n            \n            long waysToChoose = comb(studied, k) * comb(n - studied, 3 - k);\n            double survivalProb = survivalProbability(k, 3 - k);\n            favorable += waysToChoose * survivalProb;\n        }\n        \n        return favorable / totalWays;\n    }\n    \n    private double survivalProbability(int studiedInSelection, int unstudiedInSelection) {\n        // We have 3 maps: s studied and u unstudied (s + u = 3)\n        // Each player independently picks one map to discard (randomly among 3)\n        // System then randomly picks one map that wasn't discarded by anyone\n        \n        // We want probability that at least one studied map is NOT discarded by either player\n        \n        // Total possibilities: each player has 3 choices, so 3 * 3 = 9 equally likely outcomes\n        \n        int total = 9;\n        int favorable = 0;\n        \n        // Enumerate all discard combinations\n        for (int p1 = 0; p1 < 3; p1++) {\n            for (int p2 = 0; p2 < 3; p2++) {\n                // Maps are indexed 0..2, where maps 0..studiedInSelection-1 are studied\n                boolean[] discarded = new boolean[3];\n                discarded[p1] = true;\n                discarded[p2] = true;\n                \n                // Count how many maps are not discarded\n                int notDiscardedCount = 0;\n                boolean hasStudied = false;\n                \n                for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n                    if (!discarded[i]) {\n                        notDiscardedCount++;\n                        if (i < studiedInSelection) {\n                            hasStudied = true;\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                \n                // If at least one map is not discarded and at least one studied map is not discarded\n                if (notDiscardedCount > 0 && hasStudied) {\n                    // The system randomly selects one of the non-discarded maps\n                    // We need probability that a studied map is selected given this discard scenario\n                    int studiedNotDiscarded = 0;\n                    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n                        if (!discarded[i] && i < studiedInSelection) {\n                            studiedNotDiscarded++;\n                        }\n                    }\n                    favorable += (double)studiedNotDiscarded / notDiscardedCount;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        \n        return (double)favorable / total;\n    }\n    \n    private long comb(int n, int k) {\n        if (k < 0 || k > n) return 0;\n        if (k == 0 || k == n) return 1;\n        \n        long result = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n            result *= (n - i);\n            result /= (i + 1);\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-9.536738616588991e-07, -2.8371408916427754e-05, -8.4638240878121e-06, 0.0, -1.5258672647178173e-05, -8.583032467868179e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -4.6491513785440475e-06, -3.0874729418428615e-05, -1.3470558769768104e-05, -3.3378546504536644e-06, -3.0040289857424796e-05, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.00024780540843494236, -1.9073468138230965e-06, 0.0, -8.344646857949556e-07, -3.576278118089249e-07, -2.145764938177308e-06, -1.1444026313256472e-05, -6.318072337307967e-06, -1.6927575416048057e-05, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -2.2411095415009186e-05, -3.933898824470816e-06, 0.0, -7.390948667307384e-06, -0.00012861855793744326, -1.1920858014491387e-05, 0.0, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -7.033323527139146e-06, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -6.758938252460212e-05, -1.1205610462639015e-05, -7.152531907195225e-06, -9.536738616588991e-07, -3.576214658096433e-05, 0.0, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -7.152555099310121e-07, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.0371154530730564e-05, -5.364403477869928e-06, 0.0, -3.6954811548639555e-06, -1.0251946150674485e-05, -1.168244216387393e-05, 0.0, -8.22540732769994e-06, -4.887569048150908e-06, -3.576272320060525e-06, -8.630380034446716e-05, -7.033323527139146e-06, -0.000164018536452204, -3.123234637314454e-05, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -2.3841830625315197e-06, 0.0, -5.245195097813848e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -5.960446742392378e-06, -1.3232143828645349e-05, -1.311301275563892e-06, -2.3483953555114567e-05, -5.483612312673358e-06, 0.0, -7.0333235271